The AI Safety Decision Gap

A Formal Decision Theory of Capability–Assurance Gating, Irreversibility, and Coordination for Frontier Artificial Intelligence

Ouadi Maakoul

Theoretical Paper v0.5 — Revised Draft for Discussion

---

Abstract

The governance of frontier artificial intelligence confronts a structural problem that existing approaches have not adequately addressed: the question is not merely whether a system is safe, but whether the available evidence is sufficient to authorize a specific capability transition given its irreversibility, residual risk, and the current state of governance capacity. This paper develops a formal decision theory for capability transitions. We introduce a transition‑centric state representation: the central object is a mapping T_t: X_t \to X_{t+1}, not merely the current state. We define hard safety constraints that must be satisfied for any authorization to be considered, together with a normalized sufficiency score that captures the degree to which the remaining dimensions support the proposed transition. The threshold for authorization is transition‑specific and increases monotonically with irreversibility, residual risk, and capability jump. We define the Authorization Set as the set of feasible transitions that meet all constraints and achieve sufficiency above the threshold, and prove an Authorization Set Contraction Theorem: under monotonicity assumptions, increasing any risk parameter (irreversibility, residual risk, uncertainty, or capability jump) weakly shrinks the authorization set. We formulate the Authorization Problem as a constrained optimization over transitions. We then model the AI race as a two‑laboratory game and derive a clean prisoner’s‑dilemma structure, showing how a binding decision gate can shift the non‑cooperative equilibrium toward the cooperative optimum. Finally, we propose a layered governance architecture linking technical evaluation to international coordination. The paper contributes a testable formal framework rather than a philosophical warning.

Keywords: frontier AI, AI safety, capability evaluation, decision theory, assurance, coordination, governance, existential risk

---

1. Introduction

The international discourse on frontier AI safety has reached an impasse. On one side, alarmist predictions of inevitable catastrophe have proven rhetorically powerful but operationally sterile. On the other side, reliance on voluntary corporate assurance has failed to keep pace with the rate of capability advancement. The International AI Safety Report (2026) documents this precisely: while frontier safety frameworks have proliferated, their implementation remains uneven, external audits remain rare, and the relationship between pre‑deployment evaluation and real‑world safety remains poorly understood.

The central unsolved problem is not alignment in the narrow technical sense, but decision. Humanity lacks a formal procedure for determining when the available evidence concerning a frontier AI system is sufficient to authorize its next capability transition. This is the AI Safety Decision Gap: the divergence between what we can build and what we can justify building.

We must be precise about what this paper contributes. The idea of capability thresholds is not new. Anthropic’s Responsible Scaling Policy (Anthropic, 2023), OpenAI’s Preparedness Framework (OpenAI, 2023), and Google DeepMind’s Frontier Safety Framework (DeepMind, 2024) all employ if‑then commitments triggered by predefined capability levels. The European Union’s AI Act establishes risk‑based obligations. The idea that assurance should scale with risk has deep precedents in nuclear safety (LaPorte & Consolini, 1991), pharmaceutical regulation (Carpenter, 2010), and aerospace engineering (Leveson, 2011). Bostrom (2014) articulated the unilateralist’s curse for risky technological development, and Armstrong, Bostrom, and Shulman (2016) formalized race dynamics for AI. Zwetsloot and Dafoe (2019) articulated the principle of differential technological development. Recent work has proposed harmonizing safety thresholds across laboratories (Hadfield & Clark, 2023), and game‑theoretic models of AI races have been formalized (Clifton et al., 2025).

Our contribution is different. We propose a decision‑theoretic layer above existing safety thresholds. Rather than asking whether a system has crossed a capability threshold, we ask whether the evidence available at that threshold is sufficient to justify the specific transition being contemplated. This requires a formal treatment of assurance, uncertainty, risk, governance capacity, human decision capacity, and irreversibility as jointly determining an authorization decision for a particular transition, not merely for the current state.

The central shift in this paper is that authorization is a property of the transition, not merely of the system. Two systems with identical current states may warrant completely different decisions if one proposed transition is easily reversible and the other is irreversible. This reframing is crucial and distinguishes our work from existing safety frameworks.

No civilization should be forced to gamble its future because competing actors are afraid to slow down first. But we should also not pretend that slowing down is costless or that the right decision is always obvious. This paper develops the formal machinery to make these trade‑offs explicit.

