2. Related Work

This paper builds on and differentiates itself from several bodies of work.

2.1 Frontier AI Safety Frameworks

Major laboratories have adopted safety frameworks that include capability thresholds and conditional commitments. Anthropic’s Responsible Scaling Policy (Anthropic, 2023) defines AI Safety Levels (ASL) with corresponding security and deployment requirements. OpenAI’s Preparedness Framework (OpenAI, 2023) establishes risk categories (cybersecurity, CBRN, persuasion, model autonomy) with critical risk thresholds. Google DeepMind’s Frontier Safety Framework (DeepMind, 2024) identifies Capability Levels and associated mitigations. The International AI Safety Report (2026) provides a comparative analysis of these frameworks, finding that while their design has improved, implementation remains inconsistent and external verification is rare.

Our work differs from these frameworks in an important respect. These frameworks focus on whether a capability threshold has been crossed. We focus on what evidence is sufficient once a threshold has been crossed and a transition is being contemplated. The distinction is subtle but consequential: a framework may correctly identify that a system has reached a dangerous capability level, yet lack any principled procedure for determining whether the available assurance justifies proceeding from that level to the next.

2.2 Capability Thresholds and Harmonization

Recent scholarship has called for international harmonization of capability thresholds across jurisdictions (Hadfield & Clark, 2023). The argument is that divergent thresholds create regulatory arbitrage: laboratories may relocate to jurisdictions with weaker standards. Our work is complementary but distinct. Harmonization addresses the question of which thresholds should trigger scrutiny; we address the question of what evidence is sufficient once a threshold has been triggered and a transition is being proposed.

2.3 Safety Cases and Assurance

Safety cases—structured arguments that a system is safe in a given operational context—have been proposed for AI (Habli et al., 2021). Our work generalizes this approach by introducing uncertainty, irreversibility, and governance capacity explicitly into the decision function. A safety case establishes that a system is safe; our framework asks whether the available evidence is sufficient to justify a transition, given that safety cannot be established with certainty.

The broader assurance literature (Aitken et al., 2023) distinguishes between empirical assurance (based on testing), theoretical assurance (based on formal verification), institutional assurance (based on organizational processes), and historical assurance (based on track record). Our framework incorporates these distinctions through the assurance variable A_t.

2.4 AI Race Dynamics

Bostrom (2014) introduced the unilateralist’s curse: the problem that in a group of actors, the one most willing to take a risky action may determine the outcome for all. Armstrong, Bostrom, and Shulman (2016) formalized AI race dynamics, showing that actors may rationally accelerate despite collectively preferring a slower pace. Zwetsloot and Dafoe (2019) proposed differential technological development as a governance principle.

Clifton et al. (2025) provide a game‑theoretic model of regulatory competition in AI governance, demonstrating how jurisdictions may race to the bottom in safety standards. Our work extends this literature by examining how decision‑theoretic gates, combined with coordination mechanisms, can shift equilibrium outcomes. Specifically, we derive explicit conditions under which a binding decision gate moves the non‑cooperative equilibrium toward the cooperative optimum.

2.5 Human Decision Capacity

Recent work has raised concerns about the capacity of human decision‑makers to exercise meaningful oversight over increasingly complex AI systems (Bommasani et al., 2021). The concept of “scalable oversight” (Amodei et al., 2016) addresses the technical problem of supervising AI systems that may exceed human capabilities in some domains. Our contribution is to formalize this concern as a distinct variable in the decision function: even if assurance is high and uncertainty is low, decision quality may be poor if evaluators cannot meaningfully understand what they are authorizing.

