3. Formal Problem Definition

We define the following primitive objects.

Definition 1 (State Vector). A frontier AI system at time t is represented by a state vector

X_t = (C_t, A_t, U_t, R_t, G_t, H_t, I_t) \in \mathcal{X},

where

· C_t is the capability level (multidimensional in general, treated as a vector; for present purposes we summarize it as a scalar in the relevant subspace);
· A_t \in [0,1] is the assurance level (evidence, verification, independent evaluation);
· U_t \in [0,1] is the aggregate uncertainty (epistemic, behavioral, governance);
· R_t \in [0,1] is the residual risk potential (systemic risk if deployed or further developed);
· G_t \in [0,1] is the governance readiness (institutional capacity to manage risk);
· H_t is the human decision capacity, measured on a scale comparable to a required minimum H_{\text{req}}(C_t);
· I_t \in [0,1] is the irreversibility of the proposed action.

Definition 2 (Transition). A transition T_t is a mapping

T_t: X_t \to X_{t+1},

representing a proposed change from the current state to a future state. The transition may involve increasing capability, deploying a model in a new domain, increasing autonomy, or other significant changes.

Definition 3 (Transition Characteristics). Each transition T has associated characteristics:

· \Delta C(T) = C_{t+1} - C_t, the capability increment (assumed non‑negative for the transitions of interest);
· I(T) \in [0,1], the irreversibility of the transition;
· R(T) \in [0,1], the residual risk induced by the transition;
· U(T) \in [0,1], the aggregate uncertainty associated with the transition;
· A(T) \in [0,1], the assurance level specific to the transition;
· G(T) \in [0,1], the governance readiness specific to the transition.

Assumption 1 (Normalization). All characteristic values are normalized to the unit interval [0,1], with higher values of R, U, I indicating greater risk/uncertainty/irreversibility, and higher values of A, G indicating greater assurance/governance readiness.

Definition 4 (Human Decision Capacity Requirement). Let H_{\text{req}}(C_t) denote the minimum level of human decision capacity needed to meaningfully evaluate and authorize transitions from capability level C_t. We define the Governance Capacity Gap as

GCG_t = 1 - \frac{H_t}{H_{\text{req}}(C_t)}.

Interpretation: GCG_t > 0 indicates that human decision capacity is insufficient relative to the demands of the current capability level; GCG_t \le 0 indicates that it is at least minimally adequate.

---

4. Transition-Centric State Space

The central insight of this framework is that authorization decisions should be made about transitions, not merely about the current state. Two systems with identical state vectors X_t may require different decisions if the proposed transitions differ in irreversibility or risk.

Example. Consider two transitions from the same state X_t:

· Transition T_1: increase capability by a small, easily reversible amount (e.g., a minor model update with full rollback capability).
· Transition T_2: increase capability by the same amount but with persistent autonomy, replication, or irreversible deployment.

Even though C_{t+1}^{(1)} = C_{t+1}^{(2)}, the transitions differ in I(T_1) < I(T_2). Under our framework, the authorization threshold for T_2 will be strictly higher than for T_1, and the decision may differ.

This transition‑centric view is the foundation of the decision theory developed below.

---

5. Hard Safety Constraints

Before computing any sufficiency score, we impose a set of hard constraints that must be satisfied for authorization to be even considered. These constraints encode minimum acceptable conditions that cannot be traded off against other dimensions.

Definition 5 (Hard Constraints). For a transition T, the following constraints must hold:

\begin{aligned}
\text{(HC1)} \quad & R(T) \le R_{\max}, && \text{(residual risk ceiling)} \\
\text{(HC2)} \quad & U(T) \le U_{\max}, && \text{(uncertainty ceiling)} \\
\text{(HC3)} \quad & H_t \ge H_{\min}(C_t), && \text{(minimum human decision capacity)} \\
\text{(HC4)} \quad & A(T) \ge A_{\min}(T), && \text{(transition‑specific minimum assurance)} \\
\text{(HC5)} \quad & G(T) \ge G_{\min}(T), && \text{(transition‑specific minimum governance readiness)}
\end{aligned}

The absolute limits R_{\max} and U_{\max} are set by society’s risk tolerance; they are not transition‑specific. The transition‑specific minima A_{\min}(T) and G_{\min}(T) are defined in Section 7.

If any hard constraint is violated, the transition is prohibited outright, regardless of other strengths. This prevents the possibility that a high assurance score could “compensate” for an unacceptably high residual risk or a catastrophic lack of human oversight capacity.

Lemma 1 (Hard Constraints Preserve Safety Under Single‑Dimension Failure).
If a transition T violates any hard constraint, then no increase in any other dimension can make T authorized.

Proof. Immediate from the decision rule (Section 7): if any hard constraint is violated, the decision is “Prohibit” regardless of the sufficiency score. \square

---

6. Decision Sufficiency Function

For transitions that satisfy all hard constraints, we define a Decision Sufficiency Score S(T) \in [0,1] that reflects the degree to which the remaining dimensions support authorization.

Definition 6 (Product‑Form Sufficiency Score). The default specification is

S(T) = A(T)^{\alpha} \, G(T)^{\beta} \, (1 - U(T))^{\gamma} \, (1 - R(T))^{\delta},

with positive exponents \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta > 0. The default choice is \alpha = \beta = \gamma = \delta = 1, yielding

\boxed{ S(T) = A(T) \cdot G(T) \cdot (1 - U(T)) \cdot (1 - R(T)). }

This product form ensures that S(T) \in [0,1], and it is non‑compensatory in the sense that if any factor is zero, the entire score collapses to zero. No amount of assurance can compensate for zero governance readiness, and no amount of governance can compensate for maximal uncertainty.

Lemma 2 (Non‑Compensatory Property).
If any one of the factors A(T), G(T), 1-U(T), 1-R(T) is zero, then S(T) = 0. Conversely, if S(T) = 0, at least one factor is zero.

Proof. Since all factors are non‑negative and the product is zero iff at least one factor is zero, the claim follows. \square

Remark 1 (Parametric Family). The general form with exponents \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta allows sensitivity analysis: increasing an exponent makes the score more sensitive to that dimension. The product form is a special case; a min‑form S = \min\{A, G, 1-U, 1-R\} is another limiting case as exponents \to \infty. The choice of product form is a modeling decision justified by its boundedness, non‑compensatory property, and mathematical tractability.

---

7. Irreversibility and Threshold Design

The authorization threshold is transition‑specific and normalized to [0,1]. It must increase with irreversibility, residual risk, and capability increment.

Definition 7 (Authorization Threshold). We specify

\theta(T) = \min\left\{ 1, \; \theta_0 + \beta_I I(T) + \beta_R R(T) + \beta_C \Delta C(T) \right\},

where \theta_0 \in (0,1) is a baseline threshold, and \beta_I, \beta_R, \beta_C \ge 0 are sensitivity parameters.

This specification formalizes the Irreversibility Principle:

Principle (Irreversibility Principle). The more difficult a transition is to reverse, the higher the level of decision sufficiency required before it is authorized.

Formally, the threshold is monotonically increasing in I, R, \Delta C:

\frac{\partial \theta}{\partial I} \ge 0, \quad
\frac{\partial \theta}{\partial R} \ge 0, \quad
\frac{\partial \theta}{\partial \Delta C} \ge 0.

We also define a prohibition threshold \theta_{\text{prohibit}}(T) = \gamma \cdot \theta(T), with \gamma \in (0,1), used to distinguish between “restrict” and “pause/contain” decisions.

Decision Rule

Given a proposed transition T, the authorization decision is:

\text{Decision}(T) =
\begin{cases}
\text{Authorize} & \text{if all hard constraints hold and } S(T) \ge \theta(T), \\
\text{Restrict} & \text{if all hard constraints hold and } \theta_{\text{prohibit}}(T) \le S(T) < \theta(T), \\
\text{Pause} & \text{if all hard constraints hold and } S(T) < \theta_{\text{prohibit}}(T), \text{ and } I(T) < I_{\text{crit}}, \\
\text{Contain} & \text{if all hard constraints hold and } S(T) < \theta_{\text{prohibit}}(T), \text{ and } I(T) \ge I_{\text{crit}}, \\
\text{Prohibit} & \text{if any hard constraint is violated}.
\end{cases}

Here I_{\text{crit}} \in (0,1) is a critical irreversibility threshold above which the default action shifts from pause to containment.

