8. Authorization Set and Contraction Theorem

Definition 8 (Authorization Set). For a given current state X_t, the authorization set \mathcal{A}(X_t) is the set of all feasible transitions T \in \mathcal{T} that satisfy all hard constraints and achieve a sufficiency score at or above the threshold:

\mathcal{A}(X_t) = \left\{ T \in \mathcal{T} \;\middle|\; \text{HC1–HC5 hold}, \; S(T) \ge \theta(T) \right\}.

We now prove the main theoretical result of this section.

Theorem 1 (Authorization Set Contraction).
Let T_1 and T_2 be two transitions from the same current state X_t, identical in all dimensions except that T_2 has weakly higher irreversibility, residual risk, uncertainty, or capability increment (i.e., I(T_2) \ge I(T_1), R(T_2) \ge R(T_1), U(T_2) \ge U(T_1), or \Delta C(T_2) \ge \Delta C(T_1)). Suppose A(T_2) = A(T_1) and G(T_2) = G(T_1). Then, if T_2 is authorized, T_1 is also authorized. Equivalently, the authorization set is non‑increasing in each of the risk parameters I, R, U, \Delta C.

Proof. Since A and G are equal, and U(T_2) \ge U(T_1) and R(T_2) \ge R(T_1), the sufficiency score satisfies

S(T_2) = A G (1-U_2)(1-R_2) \le A G (1-U_1)(1-R_1) = S(T_1).

The threshold satisfies \theta(T_2) \ge \theta(T_1) because \theta is increasing in I, R, \Delta C and all those parameters are weakly larger for T_2. Therefore,

S(T_2) \ge \theta(T_2) \implies S(T_1) \ge S(T_2) \ge \theta(T_2) \ge \theta(T_1),

so T_1 is authorized whenever T_2 is. \square

Corollary 1 (Irreversibility‑Induced Conservatism).
Increasing irreversibility alone (holding all else fixed) weakly shrinks the authorization set.

Proof. Follows from Theorem 1 by setting only I(T_2) > I(T_1) and keeping other parameters equal. \square

This theorem formalizes the intuitive principle that more dangerous transitions require at least as much evidence to authorize as less dangerous ones, and it provides a rigorous foundation for the Irreversibility Principle.

