9. Optimal Authorization Problem

We now formulate the general decision problem faced by a governance authority.

Let \mathcal{T} be the set of all feasible transitions from the current state X_t. Each transition T \in \mathcal{T} yields an expected value V(T) (e.g., economic, scientific, or strategic benefit) and imposes constraints as defined above.

Definition 9 (Authorization Problem). The authorization problem is:

\boxed{
T^* = \arg\max_{T \in \mathcal{T}} V(T)
}

subject to

\begin{aligned}
R(T) &\le R_{\max}, \\
U(T) &\le U_{\max}, \\
H_t &\ge H_{\min}(C_t), \\
A(T) &\ge A_{\min}(T), \\
G(T) &\ge G_{\min}(T), \\
S(T) &\ge \theta(T).
\end{aligned}

The solution T^* is the transition that maximizes expected value while satisfying all safety constraints. If no transition satisfies all constraints, the governance authority should default to the status quo (no transition, or the most conservative feasible option).

This formulation transforms the problem from “is this AI safe?” to “which capability transition should be authorized, under what evidence, uncertainty, and institutional capacity?” It is a general decision‑theoretic framework applicable to any high‑risk technological transition, not only AI.

