10. Multi-Laboratory Coordination Game

10.1 Setup

We consider two laboratories, i \in \{1, 2\}, each choosing between two actions: slow (s) and accelerate (a). The payoff matrix is given in Table 1.

 Other lab: slow Other lab: accelerate
This lab: slow u_{ss} u_{sa}
This lab: accelerate u_{as} u_{aa}

We now derive the payoffs from first principles.

Let V_0 be the baseline utility from the current capability level, common to both labs. Let C_s and C_a be the capability levels achieved by a slow and an accelerating lab, respectively, with C_a > C_s. The relative advantage of lab i over lab j is C_i - C_j. The utility of lab i is assumed to be:

U_i = V_0 + \alpha (C_i - C_j) + \beta C_i - S(a_i) - \lambda R_i - \rho R_{\text{interaction}},

where:

· \alpha > 0 weights relative advantage,
· \beta > 0 weights absolute capability,
· S(a_i) is the safety cost of action a_i (with S(a) > S(s)),
· \lambda R_i is the expected cost of own residual risk,
· R_{\text{interaction}} is an additional risk that arises only when both labs accelerate (systemic interaction risk),
· \rho > 0 weights that interaction risk.

We normalize the baseline by setting V_0 + \beta C_s = 0 (or simply absorb constants). Let:

b_{\text{rel}} = \alpha (C_a - C_s) > 0, \quad
b_{\text{abs}} = \beta (C_a - C_s) > 0, \quad
c = S(a) - S(s) > 0, \quad
r = \rho R_{\text{interaction}} > 0.

Then the four payoffs are:

\begin{aligned}
u_{ss} &= 0 - S(s) = -S(s) \\
u_{as} &= b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - S(a) = b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - S(s) - c \\
u_{sa} &= -b_{\text{rel}} - S(s) \\
u_{aa} &= b_{\text{abs}} - S(a) - r = b_{\text{abs}} - S(s) - c - r
\end{aligned}

To simplify, define the baseline B = -S(s). Then:

\begin{aligned}
u_{ss} &= B, \\
u_{as} &= B + b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c, \\
u_{sa} &= B - b_{\text{rel}}, \\
u_{aa} &= B + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r.
\end{aligned}

10.2 Prisoner’s Dilemma Conditions

The game exhibits a prisoner’s dilemma if the following conditions hold:

1. Accelerate is a dominant strategy for each lab, i.e., for each lab, accelerating yields a higher payoff than being slow regardless of the other lab’s action:
   u_{as} > u_{ss} \quad \text{and} \quad u_{aa} > u_{sa}.
   From u_{as} > u_{ss}:
   B + b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c > B \implies b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} > c.
   From u_{aa} > u_{sa}:
   B + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r > B - b_{\text{rel}} \implies b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} > c + r.
   The second condition is stronger, so dominance requires:
   \boxed{ b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} > c + r. }
2. Mutual acceleration is worse than mutual slowness for both labs:
   u_{aa} < u_{ss}.
   This gives:
   B + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r < B \implies b_{\text{abs}} < c + r.
   Thus the prisoner’s dilemma occurs when:
   b_{\text{abs}} < c + r < b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}}.
   Equivalently, the interaction risk and safety cost differential are large enough to make mutual acceleration undesirable, but the relative advantage gain from being the only accelerator is large enough to make acceleration tempting.

Remark 2. The condition b_{\text{rel}} > 0 ensures that the interval (c + r - b_{\text{rel}},\, c + r) is non‑empty. If b_{\text{rel}} is very large, the lower bound becomes negative, and any b_{\text{abs}} < c + r satisfies the prisoner’s dilemma.

10.3 Global Risk

The global risk under each outcome is:

R_{\text{global}}(ss) = R_1(s) + R_2(s) + 0,

R_{\text{global}}(aa) = R_1(a) + R_2(a) + R_{\text{interaction}}.

Since R_i(a) > R_i(s) and R_{\text{interaction}} > 0, we have R_{\text{global}}(aa) > R_{\text{global}}(ss). The non‑cooperative equilibrium (mutual acceleration) is strictly more dangerous than the cooperative optimum (mutual slowness).

