11. Binding Gate Equilibrium

We now introduce a decision‑theoretic gate that must be passed before a laboratory can proceed with an accelerated transition. The gate imposes a cost on acceleration if the laboratory’s assurance is insufficient.

Let the gate be characterized by:

· A required sufficiency threshold \theta(T), as defined earlier.
· A probability of denial P(\text{denial} \mid a_i, A_i) that is increasing in acceleration and decreasing in assurance.

The modified utility of lab i becomes:

U_i = V(C_i) - S(a_i) - \lambda_i R_i - \kappa_i \cdot P(\text{denial} \mid a_i, A_i),

where \kappa_i > 0 is the cost of being denied authorization.

Proposition 1 (Gate‑Induced Equilibrium Shift).
Assume the gate is binding (i.e., it cannot be bypassed) and that P(\text{denial} \mid \text{accelerate}, A_i) > P(\text{denial} \mid \text{slow}, A_i) for any A_i. Then, for sufficiently large \kappa_i, the prisoner’s dilemma structure of Section 10 breaks down, and the non‑cooperative equilibrium may shift to mutual slowness.

Proof sketch. The gate adds an extra cost to acceleration that is not present for slowness. This effectively increases c (the safety cost differential). If the new effective c' = c + \kappa_i \cdot \Delta P becomes large enough that b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} < c' + r, then acceleration is no longer a dominant strategy. In particular, if u_{as} < u_{ss} under the gate, then slow becomes the best response to slow, and mutual slowness becomes a Nash equilibrium. \square

This proposition shows that a credible, uniform decision gate can align individual incentives with collective safety, provided the penalty for denial is sufficiently high and the gate is not subject to regulatory arbitrage.

