12. Institutional Architecture

To implement the formal framework, we propose a four‑level architecture:

```
FRONTIER AI
      │
      ▼
Technical Evaluation (Level 1)
      │
 ┌────┴────┐
 │         │
SUFFICIENT  INSUFFICIENT
 │         │
 ▼         ▼
Authorize   Independent Assurance (Level 2)
              │
              ▼
         Decision Gate (Level 3)
              │
        ┌─────┴─────┐
        │           │
      PASS         FAIL
        │           │
        ▼           ▼
   Authorize    Restrict / Pause / Contain / Prohibit
                    │
                    ▼
            International Review (Level 4)
                    │
                    ▼
            Governance Decision
```

12.1 Level 1 — Technical Evaluation

Each frontier model is subjected to capability evaluation across multiple domains: cyber‑offense, CBRN (chemical, biological, radiological, nuclear), autonomy, replication, manipulation, persuasion, and self‑improvement. The evaluation produces a capability profile C_t and an initial uncertainty estimate U_t.

12.2 Level 2 — Independent Assurance

Internal lab safety frameworks are supplemented by independent evaluators. No laboratory should act simultaneously as builder, evaluator, and judge of its own risk. Independent evaluators produce an assurance assessment A_t, based on:

· Empirical assurance: testing and observation
· Theoretical assurance: formal verification or proof
· Institutional assurance: organizational processes and incentives
· Historical assurance: track record over time

12.3 Level 3 — National Authority

National authorities receive the technical evaluation and independent assurance assessment. They compute the hard constraints and the sufficiency score S(T) for the proposed transition, and compare it to the threshold \theta(T). The default decision is restriction, not deployment. Authorization requires affirmative evidence of sufficiency.

12.4 Level 4 — International Review

National authorities report to an International AI Safety Council (IASC) with the power to:

· Classify incidents
· Order additional testing
· Recommend coordinated pauses
· Maintain a public registry of capability transitions and their assurance status

The IASC does not have enforcement power—no international body does—but it creates transparency that makes defection more costly and coordination more achievable.

