13. Simulation and Falsification Protocol

To test the framework, we propose a simulation environment with N = 10 to 50 simulated laboratories. Each laboratory chooses an action at each time step:

a_t \in \{\text{slow}, \text{maintain}, \text{accelerate}\}.

The state variables evolve according to:

C_{t+1} = C_t + \Delta C(a_t),

A_{t+1} = A_t + \Delta A(a_t),

U_{t+1} = U_t + \Delta U(C_t, A_t),

R_{t+1} = f_R(C_t, A_t, U_t, I_t).

The probability of catastrophe is:

P(\text{catastrophe}) = F(C_t, R_t, U_t, I_t, G_t, H_t).

The governance authority makes decisions according to the decision rule of Section 7.

13.1 Regimes

We compare six regimes:

Regime Description
R0 No governance
R1 Voluntary corporate frameworks only
R2 Capability thresholds (if‑then commitments)
R3 Independent assurance (external audits)
R4 Decision Sufficiency Gates (hard constraints + sufficiency score)
R5 Decision Sufficiency Gates + International Coordination

For each regime, we measure:

· P(\text{catastrophe}): probability of catastrophic outcome
· T_{\text{capability}}: time to reach a target capability level
· L_{\text{innovation}}: loss in legitimate innovation (false positives)
· F_{\text{neg}}: false negative rate (unsafe systems authorized)
· G_{\text{cost}}: governance cost

13.2 Hypotheses

We hypothesize:

· H1. Decision Sufficiency Gates (R4) reduce P(\text{catastrophe}) relative to capability thresholds alone (R2), at a modest cost in innovation.
· H2. Decision Sufficiency Gates + International Coordination (R5) achieve the lowest P(\text{catastrophe}) among all regimes with governance.
· H3. The Irreversibility Principle (higher assurance required for more irreversible actions) reduces catastrophic risk relative to a uniform threshold, without increasing false positives.
· H4. Incorporating Human Decision Capacity as a hard constraint reduces risk in high‑complexity scenarios where evaluators are overwhelmed.
· H5. The non‑compensatory product‑form sufficiency score produces lower catastrophic risk than a compensatory linear score, particularly in scenarios where one dimension of the state is severely deficient.

