Appendix A: Notation and Mathematical Preliminaries

A.1 General Notation

Throughout the appendices, we use the following notation:

· \mathbb{R} denotes the real numbers; \mathbb{R}_+ the non-negative reals.
· [0,1] denotes the closed unit interval.
· \mathbf{1}[P] denotes the indicator function, equal to 1 if proposition P is true and 0 otherwise.
· A \setminus B denotes set difference.
· A \subseteq B denotes subset (including equality); A \subset B denotes proper subset.
· \mathcal{P}(A) denotes the power set of A.
· For a function f: X \to Y, the preimage of B \subseteq Y is f^{-1}(B) = \{x \in X : f(x) \in B\}.
· A function f: X \to \mathbb{R} is monotonically increasing if x \le y \implies f(x) \le f(y), and strictly increasing if x < y \implies f(x) < f(y).

A.2 The State Space

The state space \mathcal{X} is defined as:

\mathcal{X} = \mathcal{C} \times [0,1]^5 \times \mathcal{H} \times [0,1],

where:

· \mathcal{C} is the capability space (a partially ordered set; for our purposes, a subset of \mathbb{R}^d for some d \ge 1, or a scalar interval [0, C_{\max}]),
· [0,1]^5 covers A, U, R, G, I,
· \mathcal{H} is the human decision capacity space (assumed to be a subset of \mathbb{R}_+),
· the final [0,1] covers I.

We assume \mathcal{X} is endowed with the product order: for x, y \in \mathcal{X}, we write x \le y if x_i \le y_i for all components i.

A.3 The Transition Space

A transition T is a mapping T: \mathcal{X} \to \mathcal{X}. We denote by \mathcal{T} the set of all feasible transitions from a given state X_t, where feasibility is determined by technical, physical, and institutional constraints.

For each T \in \mathcal{T}, we define the following characteristics:

· \Delta C(T) = C_{T(X_t)} - C_t (capability increment),
· I(T) \in [0,1] (irreversibility),
· R(T) \in [0,1] (residual risk),
· U(T) \in [0,1] (uncertainty),
· A(T) \in [0,1] (assurance),
· G(T) \in [0,1] (governance readiness).

We assume these characteristics are well-defined for all T \in \mathcal{T}.

A.4 Aggregation Functions

Definition A.1 (Product-form sufficiency score). For parameters \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta > 0, define:

S_{\alpha,\beta,\gamma,\delta}(T) = A(T)^{\alpha} \cdot G(T)^{\beta} \cdot (1 - U(T))^{\gamma} \cdot (1 - R(T))^{\delta}.

The default specification is \alpha = \beta = \gamma = \delta = 1, yielding:

S(T) = A(T) \cdot G(T) \cdot (1 - U(T)) \cdot (1 - R(T)).

Definition A.2 (Minimum-form sufficiency score). An alternative specification is:

S_{\min}(T) = \min\{A(T), G(T), 1 - U(T), 1 - R(T)\}.

Lemma A.1 (Relationship between product and minimum forms).
For any transition T with characteristics in [0,1],

S(T) \le S_{\min}(T).

Proof. Since all factors are in [0,1], their product is at most the smallest factor: A \cdot G \cdot (1-U) \cdot (1-R) \le \min\{A, G, 1-U, 1-R\}. \square

This lemma formalizes the intuition that the product form is more conservative than the minimum form: it penalizes even moderate deficiencies in any dimension.

