Appendix B: Formal Assumptions

We collect here all assumptions used in the main text and in the proofs.

Assumption A1 (Normalization). All characteristic values A, U, R, G, I are normalized to [0,1]. Higher values of R, U, I indicate greater risk/uncertainty/irreversibility. Higher values of A, G indicate greater assurance/governance readiness.

Assumption A2 (Monotonicity of Threshold). The authorization threshold \theta(T) is monotonically increasing in I(T), R(T), and \Delta C(T):

\frac{\partial \theta}{\partial I} \ge 0, \quad
\frac{\partial \theta}{\partial R} \ge 0, \quad
\frac{\partial \theta}{\partial \Delta C} \ge 0.

Assumption A3 (Non-negativity of Risk Parameters). I(T) \ge 0, R(T) \ge 0, U(T) \ge 0, \Delta C(T) \ge 0 for all T \in \mathcal{T}.

Assumption A4 (Feasibility of Status Quo). The "null transition" T_{\text{null}} (no change: X_{t+1} = X_t) is always feasible. It has \Delta C(T_{\text{null}}) = 0, I(T_{\text{null}}) = 0, R(T_{\text{null}}) = R_t, U(T_{\text{null}}) = U_t, A(T_{\text{null}}) = A_t, G(T_{\text{null}}) = G_t.

Assumption A5 (Hard Constraint Functions). The transition-specific minima A_{\min}(T) and G_{\min}(T) are monotonically increasing in I(T), R(T), and \Delta C(T).

Assumption A6 (Perfect Information in the Game). In the game-theoretic model, both laboratories observe the same state X_t, the same transition characteristics, and the same payoffs. There are no information asymmetries.

Assumption A7 (Risk-Averse Governance). The governance authority is risk-averse: for a given expected value V(T), it prefers transitions with lower R(T), U(T), and I(T).

