Appendix D: Proofs from Section 5 (Hard Safety Constraints)

D.1 Lemma 1: Hard Constraints Preserve Safety Under Single-Dimension Failure

Statement. If a transition T violates any hard constraint (HC1–HC5), then no increase in any other dimension can make T authorized.

Proof. The decision rule (Section 7 of main text) specifies:

\text{Decision}(T) =
\begin{cases}
\text{Prohibit} & \text{if any hard constraint is violated}, \\
\ldots & \text{otherwise}.
\end{cases}

Thus, violation of any hard constraint immediately yields "Prohibit," regardless of the sufficiency score S(T) or any other dimension. Increasing A(T), G(T), or reducing U(T), R(T) cannot change the "Prohibit" outcome because the hard constraint remains violated. \square

D.2 Properties of Hard Constraints

Lemma D.1 (Monotonicity of Hard Constraint Sets).
Under Assumption A5, the set of transitions satisfying all hard constraints is non-increasing in R_{\max}, U_{\max}, H_{\min}, A_{\min}(T), and G_{\min}(T).

Proof. Let R_{\max}^{(1)} \le R_{\max}^{(2)}. Then:

\{T : R(T) \le R_{\max}^{(1)}\} \subseteq \{T : R(T) \le R_{\max}^{(2)}\}.

Thus, tightening R_{\max} (reducing it) shrinks the set of transitions satisfying HC1. The same argument applies to HC2 (U_{\max}), HC3 (H_{\min}), HC4 (A_{\min}(T)), and HC5 (G_{\min}(T)), with the appropriate direction of the inequality. \square

---

Appendix E: Proofs from Section 6 (Decision Sufficiency Function)

E.1 Lemma 2: Non-Compensatory Property

Statement. If any one of the factors A(T), G(T), 1-U(T), 1-R(T) is zero, then S(T) = 0. Conversely, if S(T) = 0, at least one factor is zero.

Proof. Let S(T) = A(T) \cdot G(T) \cdot (1-U(T)) \cdot (1-R(T)).

(\Rightarrow) Suppose A(T) = 0. Then S(T) = 0 \cdot G(T) \cdot (1-U(T)) \cdot (1-R(T)) = 0. Similarly for G(T) = 0, U(T) = 1, or R(T) = 1.

(\Leftarrow) Suppose S(T) = 0. Since all factors are non-negative (by Assumption A1), the product is zero only if at least one factor is zero. Specifically, A(T) \cdot G(T) \cdot (1-U(T)) \cdot (1-R(T)) = 0 implies A(T) = 0 or G(T) = 0 or 1 - U(T) = 0 or 1 - R(T) = 0, i.e., U(T) = 1 or R(T) = 1. \square

E.2 Lemma: Boundedness

Lemma E.1. S(T) \in [0,1] for all T \in \mathcal{T}.

Proof. Since A(T), G(T) \in [0,1], U(T) \in [0,1], R(T) \in [0,1], we have 1 - U(T) \in [0,1] and 1 - R(T) \in [0,1]. The product of four numbers in [0,1] is also in [0,1]. \square

E.3 Monotonicity of Sufficiency Score

Lemma E.2 (Monotonicity in Dimensions).
The sufficiency score S(T) is monotonically increasing in A(T) and G(T), and monotonically decreasing in U(T) and R(T).

Proof. For A: \partial S / \partial A = G(1-U)(1-R) \ge 0 since all factors are non-negative. For G: \partial S / \partial G = A(1-U)(1-R) \ge 0. For U: \partial S / \partial U = -A G (1-R) \le 0. For R: \partial S / \partial R = -A G (1-U) \le 0. \square

E.4 Parametric Family: Sensitivity Analysis

Lemma E.3 (Effect of Increasing Exponent).
For the parametric family S = A^{\alpha} G^{\beta} (1-U)^{\gamma} (1-R)^{\delta}, increasing any exponent makes the score more sensitive to that dimension, in the sense that the elasticity of S with respect to that variable increases.

Proof. The elasticity of S with respect to A is:

\eta_A = \frac{\partial S}{\partial A} \cdot \frac{A}{S} = \alpha A^{\alpha-1} G^{\beta} (1-U)^{\gamma} (1-R)^{\delta} \cdot \frac{A}{A^{\alpha} G^{\beta} (1-U)^{\gamma} (1-R)^{\delta}} = \alpha.

Thus, the elasticity with respect to A is exactly \alpha. Increasing \alpha increases the sensitivity of S to changes in A. The same argument applies to the other exponents. \square

Lemma E.4 (Convergence to Minimum Form).
As \alpha, \beta, \gamma, \delta \to \infty with appropriate normalization, the product form converges to a multiple of the minimum form.

Proof sketch. Consider the renormalized score:

S_{\text{norm}} = \left(A^{\alpha} G^{\beta} (1-U)^{\gamma} (1-R)^{\delta}\right)^{1/(\alpha+\beta+\gamma+\delta)}.

This is the geometric mean of the factors with weights proportional to the exponents. As the exponents grow large and approach infinity, the geometric mean converges to the minimum of the factors (by the well-known result that the p-mean converges to the minimum as p \to \infty for positive values). \square

