Appendix F: Proofs from Section 7 (Threshold Design)

F.1 Monotonicity of Threshold

Lemma F.1. The threshold \theta(T) = \min\{1, \theta_0 + \beta_I I(T) + \beta_R R(T) + \beta_C \Delta C(T)\} is monotonically increasing in I(T), R(T), and \Delta C(T).

Proof. The function x \mapsto \min\{1, x\} is non-decreasing in x. The linear combination \theta_0 + \beta_I I + \beta_R R + \beta_C \Delta C is non-decreasing in each of I, R, \Delta C since the coefficients \beta_I, \beta_R, \beta_C \ge 0. Therefore, the composition \theta(T) is non-decreasing in each variable. \square

F.2 Irreversibility Principle

The Irreversibility Principle is formalized by the following lemma:

Lemma F.2 (Irreversibility Principle Formalization).
Under Assumption A2 (monotonicity of threshold), the minimum sufficiency required for authorization is monotonically increasing in irreversibility I(T). Specifically, the set of sufficiency scores that authorize a transition is non-increasing in I(T).

Proof. For fixed R, \Delta C, the threshold \theta(I) is non-decreasing in I. The set of sufficiency scores that authorize is \{S : S \ge \theta(I)\}, which is non-increasing in I (i.e., \theta(I_2) \ge \theta(I_1) implies \{S : S \ge \theta(I_2)\} \subseteq \{S : S \ge \theta(I_1)\}). \square

F.3 Comparison of Threshold Forms

Lemma F.3 (Inverse form vs. linear form).
The inverse form \theta(T) = \theta_0 (1 + \beta_I I + \beta_R R + \beta_C \Delta C)^{-1} is monotonically decreasing in I, R, \Delta C, while the linear form \theta(T) = \min\{1, \theta_0 + \beta_I I + \beta_R R + \beta_C \Delta C\} is monotonically increasing. The Irreversibility Principle requires the linear form.

Proof. For the inverse form:

\frac{\partial \theta}{\partial I} = -\theta_0 \beta_I (1 + \beta_I I + \beta_R R + \beta_C \Delta C)^{-2} < 0

since \theta_0, \beta_I > 0. This violates the Irreversibility Principle.

For the linear form:

\frac{\partial \theta}{\partial I} = \beta_I \ge 0 \quad \text{(if } \theta_0 + \beta_I I + \beta_R R + \beta_C \Delta C < 1\text{)}

or 0 if the \min\{1, \cdot\} cap binds. This satisfies the Irreversibility Principle. \square

