Appendix G: Proofs from Section 8 (Authorization Set Contraction)

G.1 Complete Statement of Theorem 1

Theorem 1 (Authorization Set Contraction).
Let T_1 and T_2 be two transitions from the same current state X_t. Suppose that for some subset of risk parameters P \in \{I, R, U, \Delta C\}, the values for T_2 are weakly larger than those for T_1 (p(T_2) \ge p(T_1) for p \in P), while for all other parameters not in P, the values are identical. Assume A(T_2) = A(T_1) and G(T_2) = G(T_1). Then, if T_2 is authorized, T_1 is also authorized. Equivalently, the authorization set \mathcal{A}(X_t) is non-increasing in each risk parameter.

Proof. We proceed by considering each risk parameter separately and then combining.

Case 1: U(T_2) \ge U(T_1), other parameters equal. Then:

S(T_2) = A G (1 - U_2)(1 - R) \le A G (1 - U_1)(1 - R) = S(T_1)

since 1 - U_2 \le 1 - U_1. The threshold \theta(T_2) = \theta(T_1) since I, R, \Delta C are equal and \theta does not depend on U. Therefore, S(T_2) \ge \theta(T_2) implies S(T_1) \ge S(T_2) \ge \theta(T_2) = \theta(T_1), so T_1 is authorized.

Case 2: R(T_2) \ge R(T_1), other parameters equal. Similarly, S(T_2) = A G (1-U)(1-R_2) \le A G (1-U)(1-R_1) = S(T_1). The threshold \theta(T_2) \ge \theta(T_1) since \theta is increasing in R. Therefore, S(T_2) \ge \theta(T_2) implies S(T_1) \ge S(T_2) \ge \theta(T_2) \ge \theta(T_1), so T_1 is authorized.

Case 3: I(T_2) \ge I(T_1), other parameters equal. The sufficiency score does not depend on I, so S(T_2) = S(T_1) = S. The threshold \theta(T_2) \ge \theta(T_1). Therefore, S \ge \theta(T_2) implies S \ge \theta(T_1), so T_1 is authorized.

Case 4: \Delta C(T_2) \ge \Delta C(T_1), other parameters equal. The sufficiency score does not depend on \Delta C, so S(T_2) = S(T_1) = S. The threshold \theta(T_2) \ge \theta(T_1). Therefore, S \ge \theta(T_2) implies S \ge \theta(T_1), so T_1 is authorized.

General case: If multiple risk parameters are larger for T_2, the argument combines by applying the above cases sequentially. Since the sufficiency score is non-increasing in U, R and independent of I, \Delta C, and the threshold is non-decreasing in R, I, \Delta C, we have S(T_2) \le S(T_1) and \theta(T_2) \ge \theta(T_1). Therefore:

S(T_2) \ge \theta(T_2) \implies S(T_1) \ge S(T_2) \ge \theta(T_2) \ge \theta(T_1),

so T_1 is authorized whenever T_2 is. \square

G.2 Corollary 1: Irreversibility-Induced Conservatism

Statement. Increasing irreversibility alone (holding all else fixed) weakly shrinks the authorization set.

Proof. Apply Theorem 1 with I(T_2) > I(T_1) and all other parameters equal. The threshold increases, the sufficiency score is unchanged, so the set of authorizing sufficiency scores shrinks. \square

G.3 Proposition: Full Authorization Set Contraction

Proposition G.1 (Full Contraction).
Let \mathcal{A}(X_t; I, R, U, \Delta C) denote the authorization set for a state with fixed risk parameters I, R, U, \Delta C. If I' \ge I, R' \ge R, U' \ge U, \Delta C' \ge \Delta C, then:

\mathcal{A}(X_t; I', R', U', \Delta C') \subseteq \mathcal{A}(X_t; I, R, U, \Delta C).

Proof. For any T \in \mathcal{A}(X_t; I', R', U', \Delta C'), we have S(T) \ge \theta(T; I', R', U', \Delta C'). By the argument in Theorem 1, S(T; I, R, U, \Delta C) \ge S(T; I', R', U', \Delta C') and \theta(T; I, R, U, \Delta C) \le \theta(T; I', R', U', \Delta C'). Therefore S(T; I, R, U, \Delta C) \ge \theta(T; I, R, U, \Delta C), so T \in \mathcal{A}(X_t; I, R, U, \Delta C). \square

