Appendix H: Proofs from Section 10 (Game-Theoretic Model)

H.1 Derivation of Payoffs

Recall the setup: two laboratories i \in \{1, 2\}, actions a_i \in \{s, a\}, payoffs:

U_i = V_0 + \alpha(C_i - C_j) + \beta C_i - S(a_i) - \lambda R_i - \rho R_{\text{interaction}} \cdot \mathbf{1}[a_1 = a, a_2 = a].

We normalize V_0 + \beta C_s - S(s) = B, where B is a constant. Let b_{\text{rel}} = \alpha(C_a - C_s), b_{\text{abs}} = \beta(C_a - C_s), c = S(a) - S(s), r = \rho R_{\text{interaction}}, and \lambda R_i = 0 for both labs (i.e., own residual risk is negligible compared to interaction risk; this assumption simplifies the derivation and can be relaxed without changing the qualitative results).

Then:

\begin{aligned}
u_{ss} &= V_0 + \alpha(0) + \beta C_s - S(s) - 0 - 0 = B \\
u_{as} &= V_0 + \alpha(C_a - C_s) + \beta C_a - S(a) - 0 - 0 \\
&= V_0 + \beta C_s - S(s) + \alpha(C_a - C_s) + \beta(C_a - C_s) - (S(a) - S(s)) \\
&= B + b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c \\
u_{sa} &= V_0 + \alpha(C_s - C_a) + \beta C_s - S(s) - 0 - 0 \\
&= V_0 + \beta C_s - S(s) + \alpha(C_s - C_a) \\
&= B - b_{\text{rel}} \\
u_{aa} &= V_0 + \alpha(0) + \beta C_a - S(a) - 0 - r \\
&= V_0 + \beta C_s - S(s) + \beta(C_a - C_s) - (S(a) - S(s)) - r \\
&= B + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r
\end{aligned}

Thus we obtain the payoff matrix:

\begin{pmatrix}
B & B - b_{\text{rel}} \\
B + b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c & B + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r
\end{pmatrix}

H.2 Prisoner's Dilemma Conditions

Definition H.1 (Prisoner's Dilemma). The game has a prisoner's dilemma structure if:

1. Dominance: For each lab, accelerate yields a strictly higher payoff than slow, regardless of the other lab's action:
   u_{as} > u_{ss} \quad \text{and} \quad u_{aa} > u_{sa}.
2. Inefficiency: Mutual acceleration yields a strictly lower payoff than mutual slowness:
   u_{aa} < u_{ss}.

Lemma H.1 (Dominance Condition).
Accelerate is a dominant strategy iff b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} > c + r.

Proof. We need u_{as} > u_{ss} and u_{aa} > u_{sa}.

For u_{as} > u_{ss}:

B + b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c > B \iff b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} > c.

For u_{aa} > u_{sa}:

B + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r > B - b_{\text{rel}} \iff b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} > c + r.

The second condition is stronger (since r > 0), so dominance requires b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} > c + r. \square

Lemma H.2 (Inefficiency Condition).
Mutual acceleration is worse than mutual slowness iff b_{\text{abs}} < c + r.

Proof. u_{aa} < u_{ss} iff B + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r < B iff b_{\text{abs}} < c + r. \square

Corollary H.1 (Existence of Prisoner's Dilemma).
The game has a prisoner's dilemma structure iff:

b_{\text{abs}} < c + r < b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}}.

Proof. Combine Lemma H.1 and Lemma H.2. \square

Remark H.1. The interval (c + r - b_{\text{rel}}, c + r) is non-empty iff b_{\text{rel}} > 0, which is true by assumption (acceleration yields some relative advantage). Thus, there always exists a range of b_{\text{abs}} values for which the prisoner's dilemma holds.

H.3 Nash Equilibrium and Global Risk

Lemma H.3 (Nash Equilibrium).
Under the prisoner's dilemma conditions, the unique Nash equilibrium is (accelerate, accelerate).

Proof. Since accelerate is a dominant strategy for each lab (Lemma H.1), the unique Nash equilibrium is (accelerate, accelerate). \square

Lemma H.4 (Global Risk Comparison).
Under the prisoner's dilemma conditions, R_{\text{global}}(aa) > R_{\text{global}}(ss).

Proof. By definition:

R_{\text{global}}(aa) = R_1(a) + R_2(a) + R_{\text{interaction}},

R_{\text{global}}(ss) = R_1(s) + R_2(s).

Since R_i(a) > R_i(s) for each i (accelerating is riskier than being slow) and R_{\text{interaction}} > 0, we have R_{\text{global}}(aa) > R_{\text{global}}(ss). \square

