Appendix I: Proofs from Section 11 (Gate-Induced Equilibrium Shift)

I.1 Full Statement and Proof of Proposition 1

Proposition 1 (Gate-Induced Equilibrium Shift).
Let the gate be characterized by a denial probability P(\text{denial} \mid a_i, A_i), where A_i is the assurance level of lab i. Assume:

1. P(\text{denial} \mid \text{accelerate}, A) > P(\text{denial} \mid \text{slow}, A) for all A \in [0,1];
2. P(\text{denial} \mid a, A) is decreasing in A for both a = s and a = a;
3. \kappa_i > 0 is the cost of denial.

Then there exists a threshold \kappa^* such that for all \kappa_i > \kappa^*, the prisoner's dilemma structure breaks down, and the unique Nash equilibrium is (slow, slow).

Proof. The modified payoff for accelerating when the other lab is slow becomes:

u'_{as} = u_{as} - \kappa_i P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a),

where A_a is the assurance level when accelerating. The modified payoff for accelerating when the other lab is also accelerating is:

u'_{aa} = u_{aa} - \kappa_i P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a).

(Note: the denial probability may depend on the state, but for simplicity we assume it is the same for both cases, or that the transition characteristics are comparable.)

The payoffs for being slow are:

u'_{ss} = u_{ss} - \kappa_i P(\text{denial} \mid s, A_s) \approx u_{ss},

since P(\text{denial} \mid s, A_s) is small or zero (a slow transition is unlikely to be denied).

For slow to be a best response to slow, we need:

u'_{ss} \ge u'_{as}.

Substituting:

B \ge B + b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c - \kappa_i P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a).

This reduces to:

\kappa_i P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a) \ge b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c.

Since P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a) > 0, this condition is satisfied for sufficiently large \kappa_i. Specifically:

\kappa_i \ge \frac{b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c}{P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a)} =: \kappa^*.

For \kappa_i > \kappa^*, slow becomes the best response to slow. By symmetry, slow is also the best response to slow for the other lab, and (slow, slow) becomes a Nash equilibrium.

To show uniqueness, we need to check whether (accelerate, accelerate) remains a Nash equilibrium. For this, we need u'_{aa} \ge u'_{sa}, i.e., accelerating is a best response to accelerating. Substituting:

B + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r - \kappa_i P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a) \ge B - b_{\text{rel}}.

Rearranging:

b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r \ge \kappa_i P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a).

For \kappa_i sufficiently large, this inequality fails, so accelerating is no longer a best response to accelerating. Thus, (accelerate, accelerate) ceases to be a Nash equilibrium.

Therefore, for \kappa_i > \kappa^*, the unique Nash equilibrium is (slow, slow). \square

I.2 Quantitative Bound on \kappa^*

Define the critical penalty as:

\kappa^* = \max\left\{
\frac{b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c}{P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a)},
\frac{b_{\text{rel}} + b_{\text{abs}} - c - r}{P(\text{denial} \mid a, A_a)}
\right\}.

For \kappa_i > \kappa^*, both conditions are satisfied, and (slow, slow) is the unique Nash equilibrium.

