Appendix J: The Authorization Set as a Topological Object

J.1 Topological Structure

Assume \mathcal{T} is a topological space (e.g., \mathcal{T} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^d for some d). Define the authorization set:

\mathcal{A} = \{T \in \mathcal{T} : \text{HC1–HC5 hold}, S(T) \ge \theta(T)\}.

Lemma J.1 (Closedness).
If the functions R(T), U(T), A(T), G(T), I(T), \Delta C(T), S(T), \theta(T) are all continuous, then \mathcal{A} is a closed subset of \mathcal{T}.

Proof. The set of transitions satisfying all hard constraints is:

\mathcal{H} = \{T : R(T) \le R_{\max}\} \cap \{T : U(T) \le U_{\max}\} \cap \{T : H_t \ge H_{\min}\} \cap \{T : A(T) \ge A_{\min}(T)\} \cap \{T : G(T) \ge G_{\min}(T)\}.

Each set \{T : f(T) \le c\} or \{T : f(T) \ge g(T)\} is closed if f and g are continuous. The intersection of closed sets is closed, so \mathcal{H} is closed.

The set \{T : S(T) \ge \theta(T)\} is closed since S and \theta are continuous. Therefore, \mathcal{A} = \mathcal{H} \cap \{T : S(T) \ge \theta(T)\} is closed. \square

Lemma J.2 (Compactness).
If \mathcal{T} is compact, then \mathcal{A} is compact.

Proof. A closed subset of a compact set is compact. \square

Compactness is important because it ensures the existence of solutions to the authorization problem (by the extreme value theorem) if the objective function V(T) is continuous.

J.2 The Decision Sufficiency Frontier

Definition J.1 (Decision Sufficiency Frontier). The Decision Sufficiency Frontier \partial \mathcal{A} is the boundary of the authorization set. It is characterized by:

\partial \mathcal{A} = \{T \in \mathcal{A} : S(T) = \theta(T)\} \cup \{T \in \mathcal{A} : \text{at least one hard constraint binds}\}.

Lemma J.3 (Characterization).
If S and \theta are continuous and \mathcal{T} is connected, then the frontier is non-empty whenever \mathcal{A} is non-empty and not equal to \mathcal{T}.

Proof. Standard result from topology: the boundary of a non-empty proper subset of a connected space is non-empty. \square

