Appendix K: Comparative Statics of the Optimal Authorization Problem

K.1 Setup

The authorization problem is:

T^* = \arg\max_{T \in \mathcal{T}} V(T) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad T \in \mathcal{A}(X_t).

Lemma K.1 (Existence).
If \mathcal{T} is compact and V is continuous, and if \mathcal{A}(X_t) \ne \emptyset, then a solution T^* exists.

Proof. The feasible set \mathcal{A}(X_t) is compact (Lemma J.2), and V is continuous, so by the extreme value theorem, V attains its maximum on \mathcal{A}(X_t). \square

K.2 Comparative Statics of the Optimal Transition

Proposition K.1 (Risk Increases Reduce Optimal Value).
If the hard constraints are tightened (e.g., R_{\max} reduced, U_{\max} reduced, H_{\min} increased, or \theta increased), then the maximum attainable value V(T^*) is non-increasing.

Proof. Tightening constraints shrinks the feasible set \mathcal{A}(X_t). The maximum of V over a smaller set is no larger than the maximum over the original set. \square

Proposition K.2 (Risk Increases Shift Optimal Choice).
Under Assumption A7 (risk-averse governance), if T^* is the optimal transition under constraints \mathcal{A}_1, and \mathcal{A}_2 \subseteq \mathcal{A}_1 is a stricter constraint set, then the new optimal transition T^{**} satisfies V(T^{**}) \le V(T^*) and, in general, has weakly lower risk parameters R, U, I, \Delta C.

Proof sketch. Since \mathcal{A}_2 \subseteq \mathcal{A}_1, T^{**} \in \mathcal{A}_1, so V(T^{**}) \le V(T^*) (by optimality of T^* on \mathcal{A}_1). The shift toward lower risk follows from risk aversion: among transitions with the same expected value, the risk-averse authority prefers those with lower risk. \square

