Appendix L: Game-Theoretic Extensions

L.1 N-Laboratory Game

Consider N \ge 2 laboratories, each choosing a_i \in \{s, a\}. The payoff for lab i is:

U_i = V_0 + \alpha \bar{C}_{-i} + \beta C_i - S(a_i) - \lambda R_i - \rho R_{\text{interaction}}(m),

where \bar{C}_{-i} is the average capability of the other labs, and R_{\text{interaction}}(m) is an increasing function of the number m of labs that accelerate.

Lemma L.1 (Dominance in N-Lab Game).
Accelerate is a dominant strategy for lab i iff:

\alpha \Delta C + \beta \Delta C > c + \rho [R_{\text{interaction}}(m) - R_{\text{interaction}}(m-1)]

for all m = 1, \ldots, N.

Proof. Similar to the two-lab case. The marginal gain from accelerating includes the relative advantage term \alpha \Delta C and the absolute capability term \beta \Delta C. The marginal cost includes the safety cost differential c and the incremental interaction risk \rho [R_{\text{interaction}}(m) - R_{\text{interaction}}(m-1)], which depends on how many other labs are accelerating. \square

L.2 Repeated Game and Trigger Strategies

Lemma L.2 (Cooperation Under Repeated Interaction).
In the infinitely repeated game with discount factor \delta, cooperation (mutual slowness) can be sustained as a subgame-perfect equilibrium via a trigger strategy if:

\delta \ge \frac{u_{as} - u_{ss}}{u_{as} - u_{aa}}.

Proof. Standard result from repeated game theory (Friedman, 1971). The trigger strategy: cooperate as long as the other lab cooperates; defect forever if the other lab defects. The incentive compatibility condition is:

\frac{1}{1-\delta} u_{ss} \ge u_{as} + \frac{\delta}{1-\delta} u_{aa},

which rearranges to the stated condition. \square

Lemma L.3 (Effect of Gate on Repeated Game).
The introduction of a gate increases the sustainability of cooperation by reducing u_{as} relative to u_{ss}, thereby lowering the required discount factor \delta^*.

Proof. The gate reduces u_{as} (the payoff from defecting against a cooperator) by \kappa P(\text{denial} \mid a, A). This reduces the numerator u_{as} - u_{ss} in the threshold condition, lowering \delta^*. Thus, cooperation is sustainable for a wider range of discount factors. \square

