Appendix M: Operationalization of Variables

M.1 Assurance A(T)

We propose measuring assurance via a structured assessment across four sub-dimensions:

A = w_1 A_{\text{empirical}} + w_2 A_{\text{theoretical}} + w_3 A_{\text{institutional}} + w_4 A_{\text{historical}},

with weights w_1 + w_2 + w_3 + w_4 = 1. Each sub-dimension is measured on a [0,1] scale using standardized protocols:

· Empirical assurance: Fraction of required tests completed, with required tests defined by capability level.
· Theoretical assurance: Availability of formal guarantees (e.g., verified bounds on behavior).
· Institutional assurance: Presence and quality of internal controls, external audits, and accountability mechanisms.
· Historical assurance: Track record of the laboratory over time (e.g., zero incidents over N years).

M.2 Uncertainty U(T)

We propose measuring uncertainty via:

U = w_E U_E + w_B U_B + w_G U_G,

where:

· U_E (epistemic) is assessed by the degree of consensus among experts and the amount of unexplained variance in model behavior.
· U_B (behavioral) is assessed by the difference between benchmark performance and real-world performance.
· U_G (governance) is assessed by the gap between governance design and implementation (see GCI below).

M.3 Residual Risk R(T)

We propose measuring residual risk via a structured risk assessment:

R = \text{expert-elicited probability of a severe incident} \times \text{expected severity of incident},

where both components are elicited via expert surveys calibrated against historical data.

M.4 Governance Readiness G(T)

We propose measuring governance readiness via the Governance Completeness Index (GCI):

G = GCI = GDI \times GII,

where GDI is the Governance Design Index (quality of framework on paper) and GII is the Governance Implementation Index (quality of application in practice), both normalized to [0,1].

M.5 Human Decision Capacity H_t

We propose measuring H_t via a combination of:

· Cognitive capacity: Expert evaluations of the ability of decision-makers to understand the system.
· Technical capacity: Availability of tools and infrastructure for monitoring and control.
· Institutional capacity: Presence of checks and balances, clear lines of responsibility, and adequate staffing.

The required capacity H_{\text{req}}(C_t) is estimated by eliciting expert judgments on the minimum capacity needed to make informed decisions at a given capability level.

M.6 Irreversibility I(T)

We propose measuring irreversibility via:

I = \max\left\{ \text{technical reversibility}, \text{institutional reversibility}, \text{de facto reversibility} \right\},

where:

· Technical reversibility: Can the transition be technically reversed (e.g., rollback to a previous model)?
· Institutional reversibility: Can the decision be reversed by the governance authority?
· De facto reversibility: Would reversing the transition be practically feasible given time, cost, and social constraints?

