Appendix O: Falsification Protocol

O.1 Conditions for Falsifying the Framework

The framework makes several empirically testable predictions. We specify conditions under which each would be falsified.

Falsification Condition 1 (Irreversibility Principle). If, in a controlled setting, more irreversible transitions are authorized at lower assurance levels than less irreversible transitions (holding all else equal), the Irreversibility Principle is falsified.

Falsification Condition 2 (Gate Effectiveness). If a binding decision gate fails to reduce the rate of catastrophic outcomes relative to a no-gate regime (in simulation or in historical data), the gate mechanism is falsified.

Falsification Condition 3 (Non-Compensatory Safety). If a non-compensatory sufficiency score does not reduce catastrophic risk relative to a compensatory score (in simulation), the non-compensatory design is falsified.

Falsification Condition 4 (Human Decision Capacity Constraint). If, in high-complexity scenarios, including H_t as a hard constraint does not reduce decision errors, the human decision capacity constraint is falsified.

O.2 Statistical Power

To detect a meaningful difference between regimes (e.g., a 20% reduction in catastrophe probability), we require at least n = 50 simulation runs per regime, based on a standard power analysis with \alpha = 0.05 and \beta = 0.20.

