Appendix P: Open Conjectures and Future Work

Conjecture 1 (Optimal Threshold Form)

For a given distribution of transitions and a loss function that is convex in false positives and false negatives, the optimal threshold \theta(T) is affine in I(T), R(T), and \Delta C(T).

Conjecture 2 (Dynamic Consistency of Gates)

In the repeated game with a gate, there exists a Markov perfect equilibrium in which all laboratories invest in assurance at the socially optimal level, provided the gate is sufficiently transparent and the cost of denial is sufficiently high.

Conjecture 3 (Superadditivity of Systemic Risk)

For N laboratories, the interaction risk R_{\text{interaction}}(m) is superadditive: R_{\text{interaction}}(m_1 + m_2) \ge R_{\text{interaction}}(m_1) + R_{\text{interaction}}(m_2) for m_1, m_2 \ge 0. Under superadditivity, the inefficiency of the non-cooperative equilibrium increases with N.

Conjecture 4 (Measurement Invariance)

The assurance measure A(T) satisfies measurement invariance across laboratories: if two laboratories have the same underlying level of assurance, the measurement procedure assigns them the same A value, regardless of their size, nationality, or other characteristics.

