Appendix R: Relationship to Other Formal Frameworks

R.1 Comparison with Safety Cases

Safety cases (Habli et al., 2021) establish that a system is safe in a given operational context. Our framework asks a different question: whether the available evidence is sufficient to justify a transition, given that safety cannot be established with certainty.

The relationship can be formalized as follows:

· A safety case establishes: P(\text{incident} \mid \text{system}) \le \epsilon for some \epsilon > 0.
· Our framework asks: Given A, U, R, G, H, is S(T) \ge \theta(T)?

A safety case contributes to A(T) (assurance), but the decision to authorize also depends on R, U, G, H, which a safety case may not address.

R.2 Comparison with Precautionary Principle

The precautionary principle states that in the face of uncertainty, actions that could cause harm should be avoided. Our framework operationalizes this principle: as U(T) increases, S(T) decreases, making authorization less likely. However, the framework does not ban all action under uncertainty; it requires the evidence to be stronger when uncertainty is high.

R.3 Comparison with Cost-Benefit Analysis

Cost-benefit analysis (CBA) compares the expected benefits of an action to its expected costs. Our framework is a constrained optimization: maximize V(T) subject to safety constraints. This is equivalent to CBA with additional constraints that cannot be traded off against benefits. The hard constraints are absolute limits that no amount of benefit can justify exceeding.

